whom he himself used to love, and used to dishonour his wife,
my mother;

I fled then far off through spacious Hellas,
and to Phthia I came, rich-soiled, mother of flocks,
to the lord Peleus;

So for your sake very many things I suffered and many labours toiled,
thinking on these things, that to me not at all did the gods a child bring to fulfilment
from myself;

and men to beseech you he has sent, the best,
having picked them out from the Achaean people, who to yourself
are dearest of the Argives;

For on them too an evil golden-throned Artemis roused,
angered because for her not at all first-fruits on the rich ground of the orchard
Oeneus had offered;

And she, angered at the heavenly race, the arrow-pourer,
roused against them a lone, wild, white-toothed boar,
which many evils used to do, haunting the orchard of Oeneus;

And him the son of Oeneus killed, Meleager,
having gathered from many cities hunter men
and dogs;

Now so long as Meleager, dear to Ares, kept fighting,
for that time it was ill with the Curetes, and they were not able
outside the wall to stay, many though they were;

and him the elders of the Aetolians besought,
and they sent the best priests of the gods,
to come out and help, promising a great gift;

And many times him besought the old man, horse-driver Oeneus,
having stepped upon the threshold of the high-roofed chamber,
shaking the close-joined doors, beseeching his son;
